<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="phytochromobilin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7170" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Phytobilins Phytobilins| are tetrapyrrole molecules that are synthesized by plants, algae and cyanobacteria. Major classes of phytobilins are: 1. |FRAME: Phycobilins Phycobilins|, which are the chromophores of |FRAME: Phycobiliproteins phycobiliproteins| |CITS: [16663825]| and some algal phytochromes |CITS: [8616213]| 2. |FRAME: Phytochromobilins Phytochromobilins|, which are the chromophores of higher plants |FRAME: Phytochromes phytochromes| |CITS: [1634523][7744741]|. |FRAME: Phycobiliproteins Phycobiliproteins| are light-harvesting accessory pigments of cyanobacteria, red algae and cryptophytes, and |FRAME: Phytochromes phytochromes| are photoreceptors that respond to red and far-red light in photosynthetic organisms. Phytochromes are dimeric proteins with each monomer covalently attached with a bilin chromophore |CITS: [16387836][20534495]|. The plant bilin chromophore, |FRAME: CPD-15004|, is synthesized from heme in the chloroplast. The assembly of phytochrome occurs in the cytoplasm. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The biosynthetic pathways for the production of the four cyanobacterial |FRAME: Phycobilins phycobilins|, as well as the |FRAME: TAX-3702| pathway for |FRAME: Phytochromobilins phytochromobilin| biosynthesis, have been elucidated. These five chromophores share a common precursor, |FRAME: BILIVERDINE|, which is the product of heme macrocycle cleavage by |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.18|. |FRAME: BILIVERDINE &quot;Biliverdin-IX-&amp;alpha;&quot;| is subsequently reduced by one of several possible ferredoxin-dependent bilin reductases. The committed step in phytochromobilin biosynthesis is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.18 &quot;heme oxygenase&quot;| (HO). Although HOs with other regiospecificities were known from bacteria and insects (see for example |FRAME: EC-1.14.99.48|), the |FRAME: TAX-3702| HOs were shown to only produce |FRAME: BILIVERDINE| (BV) isomer from heme |CITS: [19860740]|. BV is subsequently reduced in plants to (3Z)- and (3E)-phytochromobilin |CITS: [7744741]|. It&apos;s not clear whether the isomerization of (3Z) to (3E)-phytochromobilin is enzyme catalyzed or spontaneous. Different BV reductases with unique double bond specificities found in different organisms divert BV to distinct end products, such as |FRAME: BILIRUBIN| in mammals, |FRAME: Phycocyanobilins phycocyanobilin| and |FRAME: Phycoerythrobilins phycoerythrobilin| in cyanobacteria and red algae, and |FRAME: Phytochromobilins phytochromobilin| in plants and green algae |CITS: [11283349]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
