    <DataNode elementId="BILIVERDINE" textLabel="(Z,Z)-biliverdin-IX α" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="QBUVFDKTZJNUPP-BBROENKCSA-L" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="1552.216552734375" centerY="1412.616943359375" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="xue, PMN, nilo-poyanco">|FRAME: BILIVERDINE &quot;Biliverdin-IX-&amp;alpha;&quot;| is an intermediate in the degradation of heme, and the precursor for the biosynthesis of all |FRAME: Phytobilins phytobilins|, including |FRAME: Phycobilins phycobilins| and |FRAME: Phytochromobilins phytochromobilins|. Phycobilins are precursors of the chromophores of |FRAME: Phycobiliproteins phycobiliproteins|, the light-harvesting accessory pigments of cyanobacteria, red algae, and cryptophytes, and of some algal phytochromes (photoreversible light signal-transducing pigments). Phytochromobilins are precursors of the higher-plant |FRAME: Phytochromes phytochrome| chromophore |CITS: [10949378]|.</Comment>
